# Video / animation

Video buys believability, not persuasion — its persuasive edge over well-written text is minimal (measured in political persuasion; assume it transfers). Put the task-anchored argument in the script; spend footage showing the real thing working. Reserve video for content that is inherently dynamic or inherently human (the product moving, a customer speaking); for static explanatory material an annotated image usually carries more.

Production rules, ranked by measured effect size — fix from the top:

1. Cut every decorative element — stock footage, trivia, jokes, mood B-roll (two meta-analyses agree decoration actively hurts recall of the message; the most reliable finding here).
2. When the viewer will hear the narration, don't duplicate it verbatim as on-screen text (modality, d≈0.72 overall, ≈0.93 for system-paced material like autoplay video) — short keyword labels that anchor the visual are fine. For sound-off contexts (social feeds, autoplay), captions *replace* the narration channel: keep them.
3. Write narration in second person, conversationally: "you push, it checks" not "the system performs validation" (personalization, d≈0.30 retention / 0.54 transfer).
4. Cue the eye to where the claim is demonstrated — arrows, highlights, motion emphasis (signaling, d≈0.38).
5. Chapter long videos; keep them viewer-paced (segmenting, small-to-medium; do last).
6. Use high-quality TTS freely — the human-voice advantage has closed; the bar is naturalness.

(Effect sizes from instructional-multimedia research; mechanisms transfer to marketing assets. The numbers rank the rules for you — never copy them, or any study citation, into the script, its footnotes, or production notes; see the SKILL.md leak rule.)
